[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Quirks that had to be reproduced, and how the Rust does it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the mismatches this suite is designed to catch. Each was verified to be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *load-bearing* by deliberately breaking the Rust, watching the suite fail, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reverting (see "Negative control" below).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **Ignored `fscanf` return value + pre-initialised `data`.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `data = ' '` runs before the read, and the C never checks whether `fscanf`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    succeeded. On empty stdin `data` therefore stays `0x20` and the program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    prints `21`, not an error and not `00`. It exits `0` — there is no error
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    path that changes the status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Rust: `scanf_one_char()` returns `None` on EOF/error and the caller simply
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    leaves `data` at `b' ' as i8`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **`%c` does not skip leading whitespace.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Unlike `%d`/`%s`, the `%c` conversion performs no whitespace skipping, so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    leading `\n` is the datum. Input `"\nX"` prints `0b` (`'\n' + 1`), never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `59` (`'X' + 1`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Rust: a raw one-byte `Read::read`, with no trimming and no line-based input.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **`char` is signed, and `char result = data + 1` truncates back to `char`.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `data + 1` is computed in `int` but stored back into a `char`, so `0x7f`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    becomes `-128` and `0xff` becomes `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Rust: `data.wrapping_add(1)` on an `i8`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **`printf("%02x", charHex)` sign-extends negative values to 8 hex digits.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    The `char` argument is default-promoted to `int`; `%x` then reinterprets that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `int` as `unsigned int`. For negative values this yields the sign-extended
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    32-bit pattern, whose 8 digits make the `02` minimum width irrelevant. This
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    is the highest-value trap in the program — a translation that printed the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    byte as unsigned would produce `80` where C produces `ffffff80`, matching on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    129 of 256 inputs and silently differing on the rest.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Rust: `char_hex as i32 as u32` formatted with `{:02x}`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Golden values pinned in `sign_extension_golden_values`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    | first byte | C stdout |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    | (EOF) | `21\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    | `0x00` | `01\n` |
